---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site';
import { yearsExperience } from '../data/profile';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const linkRows = [
  ['GitHub', site.links.github],
  ['LinkedIn', site.links.linkedin],
  ['TryHackMe', site.links.tryhackme],
  ['Hack The Box', site.links.hackthebox],
  ['Credly', site.links.credly],
  ['Email', site.links.email],
].filter(([, v]) => v);
---

<Base
  title="About"
  eyebrow="About"
  heading="About this site"
  lede="A portfolio and an open learning plan in one place — because the plan is the more interesting artifact until the portfolio fills up."
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="narrow prose">
      <h2>Who</h2>
      <p>
        I'm <b>Dickie</b> — {yearsExperience} years in telecom network and service operations, currently
        Mobile Service Operation Officer at PT Telkom Infrastruktur Indonesia (Infranexia), covering
        Papua, Maluku, Sulawesi and Kalimantan from Makassar.
      </p>
      <p>
        My work is the operations end of carrier networks: fibre and FTTH, Metro-Ethernet, IP core and
        backhaul, DWDM, mobile broadband — and the part that decides whether any of it is actually
        reliable, which is escalation, incident coordination, and root-cause analysis on the faults that
        keep coming back. Before Telkom I certified devices at Smartfren, including factory inspection in
        Shenzhen and troubleshooting at the R&amp;D principal in Hsinchu.
      </p>
      <p>
        I am specialising into cybersecurity and agentic AI. That is not a reset — network operations is
        where security incidents are actually detected and contained, and knowing how carrier
        infrastructure genuinely behaves is context most security candidates never get. I hold CEH and
        Cisco CyberOps Associate, and I am aiming at the intersection of network/telecom security,
        detection engineering, and the security of agentic AI systems.
        <a href={`${base}/experience`}>Full experience and credentials →</a>
      </p>

      <h2>What this site is</h2>
      <p>
        Three things at once, and deliberately not separated:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <b>An open roadmap.</b> <a href={`${base}/roadmap`}>Four tracks</a> broken into phases and
          milestones, each with the artifact it must produce. Progress is visible and stored in your
          own browser, so anyone can fork the plan and use it.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>A lab tracker.</b> The <a href={`${base}/labs`}>84-room free TryHackMe path</a> plus the
          OSCP-style and post-OSCP machine lists, checkable room by room.
        </li>
        <li>
          <b>A portfolio.</b> <a href={`${base}/projects`}>Project briefs</a> and
          <a href={`${base}/notes`}>writeups</a>, filling in over time. Right now it is mostly plan and
          not much portfolio. That is the honest state of it, and the dates are on everything so the
          rate of change is visible.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Why the plan is public</h2>
      <p>
        Because an unpublished plan is one you can quietly abandon in March, and because the evidence
        that the strongest hiring and immigration routes actually want — recognition, third-party
        letters, a visible track record — is a byproduct of having been useful in public for a long
        time. It cannot be assembled in the month you need it. Starting the clock early is free.
      </p>

      <h2>Sources and credit</h2>
      <p>
        The cybersecurity lab sequence is adapted from the <i>{site.sources.trackerName}</i> — an
        Indonesian bootcamp's 100-day programme. It is credited by name and deliberately not linked,
        because it is access-restricted course material: room titles here are public TryHackMe names
        and the descriptions are written for this site. The OSCP-like and post-OSCP red team machine
        lists are LainKusanagi's publicly published lists.
      </p>
      <p>
        Market figures, certification costs, and visa criteria come from research current as of
        September 2026 and are cited inline where they carry a number. All of it moves — verify against
        the official source before spending money or making a decision.
      </p>

      <h2>How this is built</h2>
      <p>
        Astro, hand-written CSS, no framework and no build-time dependencies beyond Astro itself.
        Progress tracking is about 150 lines of TypeScript against <code>localStorage</code> — no
        accounts, no analytics, no data leaving your browser. Deployed to GitHub Pages by a GitHub
        Actions workflow on every push to <code>main</code>.
        <a href={site.repo}>Source is here</a> — fork it and replace the data files if the structure
        is useful to you.
      </p>

      {
        linkRows.length > 0 && (
          <>
            <h2>Elsewhere</h2>
            <ul>
              {linkRows.map(([label, url]) => (
                <li>
                  {label}: <a href={url!.startsWith('http') ? url! : `mailto:${url}`}>{url}</a>
                </li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </>
        )
      }

      <div class="note-box" style="margin-top: 2rem">
        <p class="small" style="margin: 0">
          <b>Fill these in:</b> the profile links live in <code>src/data/site.ts</code>. Empty values
          are hidden automatically, so add them as the accounts exist rather than leaving placeholders.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
